.TH ECOLABTEST 1
.SH NAME
ecolabtest \- check every Danby two-species model written as an ecolab(2) script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ecolabtest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ecolabtest
reads the
.B SPEC
constant out of every model under
.B /appl/danby
that calls
.BR ecolab->run ,
so that what is checked is the string that ships, and then checks that
the spec parses, that it has two states, that no rate is a NaN at the
start, and that the model is at rest at the equilibrium its own spec
declares.
.PP
The last is the check that matters.  A two-species phase plot marks a
fixed point and draws trajectories that are supposed to circle or
approach it; a fixed point the equations do not actually fix is a lie on
the screen, and it is an easy one to tell, because the equilibrium is
written by hand as an expression in the parameters and nothing else
forces the two to agree.  A model whose equilibrium is the origin is
skipped, since every model of this shape fixes the origin and the check
would pass without saying anything.
.PP
Two models are checked differently.
.IR Periodicvolterra 's
birthrate varies with time, so the point it marks is the
.I mean
equilibrium: the rates there are non-zero at
.BR t =0
and zero a quarter period later, and both halves of that are checked.
.IR Maypredator 's
predators have no carrying capacity of their own, so with the prey gone
they die off at a rate set by one parameter, which is checked instead.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ecolabtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR orblabtest (1),
.IR trajlabtest (1),
.IR poplabtest (1),
.IR mechlabtest (1),
.IR ecolab (2)
.SH BUGS
Equilibria are checked at the declared point only, not along a
trajectory, so a term that vanishes there and is wrong elsewhere would
pass.  Nothing checks that the equilibrium is the one a reader would
call interesting, only that it is one.
